Push camera snapshots to FinBlade; add a single-frame endpoint

Adds a sixth stream: one annotated JPEG per online camera, base64 in the
payload, every 30s by default.

Deliberately thumbnails rather than video. Continuous MJPEG is 20-40 Mbit/s PER
VIEWER at 1080p with no fan-out, because frames are re-encoded as JPEG at ~10x
the size of the equivalent H.265 - unusable across a WAN and disproportionate
for a dashboard tile. One frame per camera per 30s is ~2 KB/s, four orders of
magnitude less, and enough for a tile, incident context, or a "what did it look
like" view. Genuine live video stays a PULL over a link that can carry it.

New GET /api/v1/cameras/{id}/snapshot returns a single JPEG. The camera already
holds the frame it encoded for the stream, so this is essentially free, whereas
opening an MJPEG stream to grab one frame costs a held connection and a
continuous encode.

Snapshots are rate-limited independently of the 5s telemetry tick, and only
ONLINE cameras send: a snapshot from an offline camera is a stale frame from
before it dropped, which is worse than none because it looks live.

Fixes an env var collision I introduced with the auth work: FINBLADE_API_KEY was
serving as BOTH the inbound key callers present to us and the outbound
credential we present to FinBlade. Those are different secrets held by different
parties - sharing one variable would hand FinBlade the key that unlocks this API,
and rotating either would silently break the other. Outbound is now
FINBLADE_OUTBOUND_KEY.

Also fixes a fragile liveness check found by the tests: the filter keyed on
effective_state, which only the SQLite store computes, so snapshots would have
silently never sent on any other backend. Now falls back to the raw state and
requires a recent heartbeat either way.

Verified against a live camera and a stand-in receiver: the snapshot endpoint
returned a valid 59 KB JPEG, and 7 snapshots were pushed and decoded intact
(62,495 bytes declared and received, valid JPEG magic).

398 tests, 6 new.

